  "response": "When storm damage is ambiguous, meteorologists reconcile observational streams of very different quality: what people saw, what radar sampled aloft, and what the ground records. In the case examined here, the National Weather Service dispatched a damage survey team to the affected county. The team's task was not to arbitrate between witnesses and instruments by preference but to assemble physical evidence that either the human or the remote-sensing account would have to accommodate.\n\nThe human account was vivid. Several eyewitnesses reported seeing a funnel cloud over the county. Sightings of this kind are what produce a well-documented pattern in severe-weather verification, namely that eyewitnesses sometimes mistake scud clouds or rain shafts for funnel clouds, and it was this tendency that caused the National Weather Service to dispatch the survey team. Ragged low cloud beneath a gust front, or a dense curtain of precipitation backlit by lightning, can take on a tapered, pendant shape in poor light. Despite those sightings, Doppler radar velocity data showed no tight rotational couplet over the damage area, meaning no adjacent maxima of inbound and outbound radial velocity of the kind that marks a mesocyclone or tornado vortex signature.\n\nForecasters give the absence of a couplet considerable weight, as shown by the fact that Doppler radar directly measures wind speed at ground level along a storm's entire path. The velocity field therefore spoke against a vortex. By contrast, the competing interpretation, driven by the witness reports, holds that a tornado touched down within the damage swath. Either that is so, or no tornado touched down within the damage swath; the two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. The survey exists to decide which.\n\nReflectivity data supplied a second radar line of evidence. Radar reflectivity data showed a bow echo moving across the county, the arched, forward-surging line segment associated with a strong rear-inflow jet and organized outflow at the surface. On the ground, surveyors mapped the direction in which trees fell along the damage swath, work that came after the team's dispatch to the county. Fall patterns are diagnostic because the two wind regimes leave different signatures in debris: tornadic damage tends toward convergent or crisscrossing tree falls, while outflow lays trees down in broadly parallel or fan-shaped divergent sets. Whereas the bow echo reflects a linear surge of air, downburst winds rotate cyclonically around a central point.\n\nThe survey must then account for the structural damage itself. At least one of the following holds, perhaps both: straight-line downburst winds damaged structures in the swath, or tornadic winds damaged structures in the swath. Surveyors assign wind estimates by matching observed failures against damage indicators and degrees of damage, such as roof decking removed, walls collapsed, or trees snapped at the trunk, and then reading off the expected wind range for each. The tornadic structural damage is what led to the peak winds in the swath being estimated at 100 mph. In other words, the maximum estimated wind speed along the swath was 100 miles per hour, a value achievable by either mechanism and therefore not decisive on its own. Synthesizing the radar velocity record, the bow-echo reflectivity structure, and the tree-fall map, the NWS survey team concluded that the damage was caused by a straight-line downburst. That conclusion settles the tension between the funnel-cloud reports and the velocity data: the witnesses observed something real in the sky, but the ground evidence and the radar together identify the damaging wind as outflow rather than a vortex.",
